Statement of Novelty
Meat and bone meal is a common biowaste. In traditional treatment methods, considerable amount of valuable C and N which are locked in the meat and bone meal are unavoidably lost from the circle. In this study, the effect of batch feeding treatment with meat and bone meal (MBM) as substrate for black soldier fly larvae (BSFL) was investigated. The results showed that changing batch feeding times can reduce greenhouse gas emission and improve carbon and nitrogen recycle from biowaste to larval biomass. Our results provide reference for using meat and bone meal as substrate for BSFL batch feeding and lay a certain technical foundation for the large scale of black soldier fly larvae bioconversion of meat and bone meal.
Introduction
There will be more than 9 billion population in the world by 2050 at the current growth rate [1]. With the increasing population, the global meat production will be doubled from the 229 million tons in 1999/2001 to 465 million tons in 2050 [2].The intensive meat production will result in increasing slaughter wastes. Meat and bone meal (MBM) is a kind of animal waste from meat processing industry, and MBM is classified as high risk material [3]. MBM is composed of inedible livestock bodies, bones, internal organs, and other wastes, which is subjected to high temperature and high pressure processing at 133 °C for 20 min at 3 bars, and then it is crushed [4]. MBM contains a large number of elements such as C (30%), N (8%), P (5%), and Ca (10%), mainly in slowly-soluble organic forms [5]. Due to its rich nutrient elements, MBM tends to be used as crop fertilizer and solid amendment to help the growth of plants [6]. However, in traditional treatment methods, considerable amount of valuable C and N which are locked in the MBM are unavoidably lost from the circle. These lost C and N will be leached into water system or will be emitted into the air (such as GHG or NH3, etc.). The loss of
nutrients causes a decline in the quality of the end-product and environmental pollution, which prompts researchers to explore alternative treatment of MBM, such as MBM bioconversion by insect composting. In order to enable MBM to be sufficiently utilized, it is necessary to develop economic and environment-friendly alternatives. Hence, an insect is introduced to accelerate the C and N recycle and to transfer the nutrients of MBM into insect. Black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens L.) larvae (BSFL) composting is an ecologically- green technology, which
can convert various organic wastes such as livestock manure, food waste, and sludge, and the conversion residues can be utilized as bio-fertilizer. In particular, compared to other waste treatment technologies, waste-treating BSFL provides protein- and fat-rich biomass, which can be further used for biodiesel production and animal feeding, and also provides other by-products such as chitin [7]and antibacterial peptide [8]. All of these showed the potential of BSFL to recycle organic waste. GHG and NH3 emission is unavoidable due to many kinds
of physicochemical factors in traditional composting. The percentage of N loss during the traditional composting varies from 19 to 77% with NH3 emission as the main total N
loss [9]. Besides, about 14–51% of the initial carbon content is lost in the form of greenhouse gases (GHG), particularly CH4 and CO2 emission [10]. Using global warming potential
(GWP) to estimate GHG in composting [11], GHG contains CO2, CH4 and N2O. In particular, GWP in CH4 and N2O
was 34 and 298 folds as much as that in CO2, respectively
[12]. The emission of NH3 is the most important pathway
of N loss in composting. Literature has indicated that the BSFL composting exhibits some advantages in that the GHG and NH3 emission in BSFL composting treatment was lower
than that in traditional composting of organic waste [13] [13]. BSFL generally exhibited a S-shaped growth curve, and BSF at different stages has different growth rates [15]. Hence, the substrate input increases with the advancing larval stage. Feeding system has been reported to have a significant influence on the larval weight [16]. However, the increasing batch feeding times lead to the labor input increase accordingly. Hence, BSFL was introduced to treat MBM by batch feeding method, the certain amount of substrate was batch fed to BSFL to explore the conversion of the substrate and the emission of GHG and NH3. The aim of the
present study was: (1) to evaluate GHG and NH3 emission
in BSFL composting treatment under different MBM batch feeding times; (2) to determine the optimal batch feeding times and BSFL conversion efficiency; and (3) to use BSFL as a bioreactor to transfer organic waste for converting the C and N from the initial substrate into larval biomass.
Materials and Methods
Materials
The experiment was performed at Huazhong Agricultural University (HZAU). The meat and bone meal was obtained from Wuhan Huanbao agriculture corporation, and the rice bran (RB) was from the experimental field of HZAU. The BSFL (Hermetia illucens, Wuhan strain) was provided by the State Key Laboratory of Agricultural Microbiology of HZAU, Wuhan, China. The RB was used as a kind of


