Carbon and Nitrogen Balance During BSFL Composting
Evaluation of Carbon Transformation
To evaluate C balance, total organic carbon (TOC) in initial substrate and final substrate was measured. The cumulative C loss through CO2 emission accounted for 13.30–36.76% of
the initial TOC due to BSFL and microbial metabolism in a natural process. CO2 emission causing C loss was increased
with the increased batch feeding times, which was attributed to the fact that the aeration rate and microbial activity was increased with the movement of BSFL in the substrate. In addition, BSFL activity decreased CH4 emission,
and almost eliminated the effect of CH4 (0.00–0.02%). The
BSFL obtained its nutrients by converting the substrate. In substrate conversion, larva-C accounted for 7.82–18.55% of the total carbon. Furthermore, the BSFL in the T5 treatment exhibited the highest C content, indicating that more C was transferred to the BSFL from substrate, which meant a higher C conversion efficiency. The residue-C accounted for 10.55 to 32.45% of total C in 5 treatment, and the residue C in T1 treatment displayed the lowest C content (10.55%), and the other C losses in this treatment accounted for 68.33%, indicating that most C was lost in other forms during treatment process and less C could be transformed into the gases or the BSFL biomass.
