Characteristics of Final Products
Table 3 displayed the fresh larval biomass and final residue weight. Significant differences (P < 0.05) in fresh larval mass were found among the five treatments. The highest larval weight was observed in T5 treatment (147.11 ± 19.10 g), followed by T4 (125.27 ± 9.33 g), T3 (122.50 ± 11.05 g), T2 (99.23 ± 3.32 g), and T1 (66.41 ± 11.45 g). However, in all the 5 treatments, residue weight decreased with the increase in batch feeding times, implying that increasing the batch feeding times could accelerate the decomposition of substrates. Table 3 also showed that the residue moisture content. Little difference in residue moisture was observed among T3, T4, and T5, all of which was about 48%, while in T1 and T2 treatments, residue moistures were 57.78% and 55.63%, respectively, indicating that there was a negative correlation between the batch feeding times and moisture content. Table 4 showed the C and N conversion efficiency in the harvested larvae and residue. In all the 5 treatments, the C + N in the larvae increased from 6.79 g/kg to 27.28 g/kg, with the increased batch feeding times, while the C + N in the residue ranged from 30.58 g/kg to 72.72 g/kg and the highest C + N (72.72 g/kg) was observed in the T4 treatment residue. The C + N conversion efficiency was increased with the increased batch feeding times in four treatments with exception of the fluctuation in T3. The maximum C + N conversion efficiency (31.17%) was observed in T5 (Table 4). To calculate GHG, the GHG was converted to CO2 equivalent.
