Practical Application and Future Research Perspective
With the increasing global consumption of livestock and poultry meat products, MBM, as a by-product of livestock and poultry products, also increases in quantity. Due to its rich nutritional content, MBM is often used as a soil amendment. Considering this, the present study made the first attempt to evaluate the MBM as a substrate for batch feeding BSFL. BSFL composting is an emerging organic waste treatment method that can convert MBM into biofertilizer and insect proteins. The times of batch feeding explored in this experiment played a role in increasing the larval biomass, or reducing the GHG production during BSFL composting process. This study will provide data for the future largescale BSFL composting and protein production.
Conclusion
This study used MBM as a raw material for BSFL composting for the first time, BSFL acted as a green reactor to converses organic waste. Our results indicated that batch feeding times had a great impact on GHG and NH3 emissions.
The accumulative emission amount of CH4, N2O, CO2, and
NH3 was the lowest in the one-time batch feeding treatment,
which was 4.89 mg/kg DM, 0.27 mg/kg DM, 174.22 g/kg DM, and 5.29 mg/kg DM, respectively. With the increase in the batch feeding times, the highest GHG cumulative amount was achieved in T5 treatment. The total cumulative CO2 emission amount was found to be the highest in the
T5, namely, five-time batch feeding treatment, which was consistent with the largest total weight of fresh BSFL. Our data indicated that one-time batch feeding BSFL treatment achieved the lowest GHG, and that five-time batch feeding achieved the highest BSFL biomass. Hence, MBM treatment by BSFL can reduce GHG emission, or obtain high biomass to provide bioavailable protein and lipids for animal feeding. This study provides a novel information on the use of
